- How would you tune thresholds to minimize false positives for bursts?
- How would you validate end-to-end remediation without impacting real users?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design dynamic, policy-driven routing with on-edge latency constraints, multi-tenant safeguards, and auditable provenance. It also probes risk scoring, drift detection, and rollback semantics under real-world operational pressures.

## Key Concepts

- Contextual bandits for real-time routing decisions
- Per-tenant safety profiles and data locality constraints
- Latency budgets and SLA adherence (p95 targets)
- Drift detection and automated rollback to baseline
- Tamper-evident, cryptographically signed per-tenant audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
function selectVariant(context, stats) {
  // context: {tenant, latency, safetyProfile}
  // stats: {risk, base, tuned, safety}
  if (Math.random() < 0.1) return 'base';
  if (stats.risk > 0.6) return 'safety-guarded';
  const scores = { base: stats.base, tuned: stats.tuned, safety: stats.safety };
  return Object.keys(scores).reduce((a,b) => scores[a] > scores[b] ? a : b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you prevent feedback loops from online learning contaminating production signals?
- What telemetry and signing strategy ensures tamper resistance without leaking tenant data?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical skills in enforcing privacy, security, and auditability in a multi-tenant LLM-ops gateway. It requires concrete architecture and measurable guarantees beyond high-level design.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Deterministic validation and replayability
- PII detection and redaction
- Prompt-injection guardrails
- Latency budgeting and tamper-evident logging

## Code Example

```javascript
function validatePrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // normalize
  let p = prompt.trim();
  // redaction rules (pseudo)
  policy.redactionPatterns.forEach(r => p = p.replace(r, 'REDACTED'));
  // injection guard (very simple placeholder)
  const blocked = policy.injectionPatterns.some(pat => new RegExp(pat, 'i').test(p));
  return { valid: !blocked, redacted: p };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy coverage across tenants with different privacy rules?
- How would you handle policy updates without affecting in-flight prompts?

